---
id: opendb-architecture
type: paper
title: "A public-domain knowledge base without a server"
summary: How OpenDB stores articles, media and papers as reviewable text files and serves them as a static site with instant client-side search.
abstract: Open knowledge projects usually depend on a database server, which brings hosting costs, operational risk and a single point of failure. We describe OpenDB, a public-domain (CC0) knowledge base that needs none. Each entry is a plain text file with a short header of facts followed by Keydown content with LaTeX math. A build step compiles all entries into one index file that a static website loads once and searches entirely in the browser. Contributions are prepared in the browser, saved locally right away, and submitted as reviewable changes to a public repository, so history, moderation and backups come from version control. We discuss the trade-offs of this design, including slower publication and the size limits of a single index, and why dedicating everything to the public domain simplifies reuse.
tags: [open access, digital libraries, web]
categories: [cs.DL, cs.IR]
created: 2026-10-27
updated: 2026-10-27
authors: [Radii Office contributors]
license: CC0-1.0
